        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
        ":question/problem": "Which of the following diagrams gives the slope field at the points indicated for the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=1x+(2)y?$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "We have to sketch the slope field at the points $(-2,1)$,$(1,1)$,$(2,1)$.\n\nTo sketch the slope field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the right-hand side of the differential equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(-2,1)}=1(-2)+(2)(1)=0, \\\\  & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(1,1)}=1(1)+(2)(1)=3, \\\\  & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(2,1)}=1(2)+(2)(1)=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting up our points and drawing our line segments, we get the following diagram.\n\n![](generate/scratch/17592186172038/plots/e11ffd8a491adc66ae6058fe8fbdc8c9f57efaf17ca5a7544bcbeb90f8606eb0.png)",
